      "title": "Luxonis: New Silver OSRA Member and a New Product Release (that you can win!)",
      "link": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/luxonis-new-silver-osra-member-and-a-new-product-release-that-you-can-win/59245",
      "summary": "Today Luxonis has entrusted us with sharing two announcements with the community. The first is that Luxonis has strengthened its commitment to the ecosystem by increasing their OSRA membership from Supporter to Silver . Their enhanced membership matches the company’s growth and success, and is evidence of the importance of robotics to Luxonis. “We believe robotics should be accessible to anyone with an idea and the drive to build it. OSRA shares our commitment to open source and collaboration, and we’re proud to join that effort.” –Max McLaughlin, Director of Product & Growth, Luxonis The second is the release of the Luxonis OAK 4 FFC vision computer! Place up to five cameras anywhere on your robot and the tiny (70mm x 53mm) OAK 4 FFC handles sync, recalibration, depth, and AI between them all. It’s just released today and you can learn all the details at the Luxonis site , as well as check out an open source reference design for it on GitHub . To celebrate these two happy announcements, Luxonis is offering a free OAK 4 FFC developer kit for a member of the Open Robotics community. The kit—which comes with the board and sensors—will sell for $849 USD and will ship them in November.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T15:48:46+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-27T15:48:46+00:00",
      "source": "ROS2 Discourse",
      "section_hint": "로보틱스",
      "articleText": "Luxonis: New Silver OSRA Member and a New Product Release (that you can win!) - OSRA Announcements and News - Open Robotics Discourse Luxonis: New Silver OSRA Member and a New Product Release (that you can win!) OSRA Announcements and News vmb October 27, 2026, 3:48pm 1 Today Luxonis has entrusted us with sharing two announcements with the community. The first is that Luxonis has strengthened its commitment to the ecosystem by increasing their OSRA membership from Supporter to Silver . Their enhanced membership matches the company’s growth and success, and is evidence of the importance of robotics to Luxonis. “We believe robotics should be accessible to anyone with an idea and the drive to build it. OSRA shares our commitment to open source and collaboration, and we’re proud to join that effort.” –Max McLaughlin, Director of Product & Growth, Luxonis The second is the release of the Luxonis OAK 4 FFC vision computer! Place up to five cameras anywhere on your robot and the tiny (70mm x 53mm) OAK 4 FFC handles sync, recalibration, depth, and AI between them all. It’s just released today and you can learn all the details at the Luxonis site , as well as check out an open source reference design for it on GitHub . To celebrate these two happy announcements, Luxonis is offering a free OAK 4 FFC developer kit for a member of the Open Robotics community. The kit—which comes with the board and sensors—will sell for $849 USD and will ship them in November. To enter to win the kit, all you need to do is comment on this post and tell Luxonis what you’d do with it. You have a week (until November 3rd), after which the Luxonis team will choose their favorite comment. That author will win the kit! We’re grateful to Luxonis for the continuing support and look forward to working with them even more closely to increase the open source robotics ecosystem. 3 Likes Carson_Johnston October 27, 2026, 4:26pm 2 Excited to see who wins! nathan October 27, 2026, 5:08pm 3 This looks amazing, I wish I get this !!! I’m currently using the OAK-D pro wide on a custom built drone to do site inspections in low light as a part of my PhD research, but we are struggling with flight time since we are using the RPi with a hailo accelerator to do our computing and my setup is a bit bulky. I would love to use something like your dev kit which looks fairly lightweight and would help me shave some grams of my drone while also getting access to a multi view of the surroundings. My current setup only has a front facing camera which I pass through my custom vSLAM algorithm to enable better tracking and mapping in low light. shaposhn October 27, 2026, 7:16pm 4 Super exciting! If we win your dev kit we would use it in our free-flying robot called Orb launching to the International Space Station in May 2027. We already use your Luxonis OAK-D Pro W camera in prototyping, and individual modules could make it easier for us to have a full collision avoidance system and localization on all sides of the robot. A unique problem when you can float in any direction in zero-gravity Related topics Topic Replies Views Activity Luxonis - Community input for next generation cameras ROS General 34 2455 November 5, 2022 OpenCV AI Kit (OAK) Projects 40 20622 November 1, 2022 Luxonis Solutions + Luxonis Agent Toolkit ROS General 0 62 September 8, 2026 Luxonis - Robotics Access for Everyone: rae ROS General 5 1664 December 15, 2022 ROS Lead - Luxonis (Prague, Czech Republic / Ljubljana, Slovenia) Jobs contracting , fulltime , ros2 1 312 February 10, 2026",
