      "title": "webframe: evaluated as a scraper and a sandbox",
      "link": "https://simonwillison.net/2026/Oct/26/webframe-evaluation/",
      "summary": "Research: webframe: evaluated as a scraper and a sandbox webframe is a neat little project from Jeff Lindsay which lets you \"run a web page like a program\" - you point it at a URL or file and it executes it in a hidden headless web view and outputs console.log() to standard output and any exceptions to standard error. It's written in Go on top of the glaze library, which provides access to a system browser (\"WKWebView on macOS, WebKitGTK on Linux, WebView2 on Windows\") so it doesn't need to bundle its own browser engine. It reminds me of my own shot-scraper javascript tool, albeit in a much smaller package that doesn't depend on Playwright. I had Claude Opus 5.5 put it through its paces and prepare a research report for me. Here's the prompt I used: Explore https://github.com/tractordev/webframe try running it and put it though its paces, build a few exploratory prototypes, then evaluate it as a potential sandboxing tool and as a tool for scraping web pages using the same patterns encouraged by the \"shot-scraper JavaScript\" command (see simonw/shot-scraper on GitHub, docs directory) Tags: browsers , go , javascript , jeff-lindsay , shot-scraper",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T16:15:00+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-26T16:15:00+00:00",
      "source": "Simon Willison",
      "section_hint": "AI",
      "articleText": "Research: webframe: evaluated as a scraper and a sandbox Simon Willison’s Weblog Newsletter Sponsored by: WorkOS Airlock — intent-based access control for AI agents. Request early access 26th October 2026 Research webframe: evaluated as a scraper and a sandbox — An evaluation of webframe found it to be a fast, lightweight browser-JavaScript runner suitable for scraping, with performance comparable to shot-scraper, but with important rough edges including CSP failures, a 0×0 viewport, hanging loads, missing HTTP metadata, and limited browser controls. webframe is a neat little project from Jeff Lindsay which lets you \"run a web page like a program\" - you point it at a URL or file and it executes it in a hidden headless web view and outputs console.log() to standard output and any exceptions to standard error. It's written in Go on top of the glaze library, which provides access to a system browser (\"WKWebView on macOS, WebKitGTK on Linux, WebView2 on Windows\") so it doesn't need to bundle its own browser engine. It reminds me of my own shot-scraper javascript tool, albeit in a much smaller package that doesn't depend on Playwright. I had Claude Opus 5.5 put it through its paces and prepare a research report for me. Here's the prompt I used: Explore https://github.com/tractordev/webframe try running it and put it though its paces, build a few exploratory prototypes, then evaluate it as a potential sandboxing tool and as a tool for scraping web pages using the same patterns encouraged by the \"shot-scraper JavaScript\" command (see simonw/shot-scraper on GitHub, docs directory) Posted 26th October 2026 at 4:15 pm Recent articles Battle Drones - a vibe coded RTS with audio-based multiplayer - 20th October 2026 Hello Python 3.15, goodbye 3.10, and now I can use 3.11 - 12th October 2026 A new feature for my blog, built using my voice - 9th October 2026 Claude Haiku 5.5 - 7th October 2026 This is a beat by Simon Willison, posted on 26th October 20